<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Test project for shared/PowerFramework.Contracts, the published cross-service boundary:
    Proto/common.v1.proto, Proto/dataservices.v1.proto, Proto/persistence.v1.proto,
    OpenApi/security.v1.yaml and OpenApi/gateway.v1.yaml.

    HAND-AUTHORED ON PURPOSE. `dotnet new xunit3` does not exist in SDK 10.0.302 and
    `dotnet new xunit` scaffolds the xunit v2 line with an older test SDK and coverage package,
    so templating this project would silently downgrade the test stack.

    Everything else is inherited from the repository-root Directory.Build.props by directory
    walk-up: net10.0, C# latest, nullable enabled, implicit usings enabled, warnings as errors,
    .NET analysers, and central package management. Those are mandatory quality gates, so they
    are deliberately NOT restated here and must never be relaxed per project. Adding NoWarn, or
    turning off warnings as errors, or disabling nullable to make a test compile would be a
    violation rather than a fix: the fix belongs in the offending test file. Package versions
    live in the repository-root Directory.Packages.props, which is why every PackageReference
    below is versionless apart from the one documented override.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- MANDATORY for xunit.v3, which runs tests from a self hosting executable. Without Exe the
         project still compiles but no test is discovered, and the coverage gate then reads a
         Cobertura report that contains no data. This is the single property that distinguishes a
         v3 test project from a v2 one. -->
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Test stack and OpenAPI object model (versions from Directory.Packages.props)">
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" />
    <!-- Produces coverage.cobertura.xml, the exact artifact the 80 percent line coverage gate is
         measured from, when the test command collects XPlat Code Coverage. -->
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" />

    <!-- Centrally pinned at 2.11.0, and that pin is mandatory: 2.0.0 carries NuGet advisory
         NU1903, while the 3.x line breaks the build because the 10.0.x OpenAPI source generator
         is compiled against the 2.x object model and emits CS0200. This project is where that
         pin gets its regression guard, which is why it takes the reference directly. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.OpenApi" />

    <!--
      WHY THIS PACKAGE IS HERE, AND WHY IT CARRIES VersionOverride INSTEAD OF Version.

      Microsoft.OpenApi 2.11.0 ships JSON and YAML *writers* plus a JSON *reader* only. Its
      assembly exposes OpenApiJsonReader, OpenApiJsonWriter and OpenApiYamlWriter, and no YAML
      reader of any kind (verified by inspecting the published package). Both contract documents
      are YAML, so loading and validating them against the pinned object model is physically
      impossible without this reader.

      Microsoft.OpenApi.YamlReader publishes an exactly version locked 2.11.0 whose dependencies
      are Microsoft.OpenApi 2.11.0, SharpYaml 2.1.4 and System.Text.Json 8.0.5, all MIT licensed.
      Being locked to 2.11.0, it cannot drag the mandatory pin off that version, and
      System.Text.Json 8.0.5 already sits in Microsoft.OpenApi's own closure, so SharpYaml is the
      only net addition to the dependency graph. None of the three carries a NuGet advisory at
      these versions, so restore stays audit clean, which matters because warnings are errors.

      VersionOverride, never a bare Version attribute (which trips NU1008 under central package
      management), supplies the version for this one project without editing the repository-root
      Directory.Packages.props. That is what keeps the promise that this project restores and
      builds from a clean checkout with no edit to any file outside its own folder.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.OpenApi.YamlReader" VersionOverride="2.11.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The subject under test, and the only supplier of the generated Protobuf message types and
         of the gRPC service base classes and client stubs (generated with GrpcServices Both).
         Google.Protobuf arrives transitively through it, so it is deliberately not referenced
         explicitly here: an explicit reference would risk a version conflict. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\PowerFramework.Contracts\PowerFramework.Contracts.csproj" />

    <!--
      NOT A STRAY REFERENCE, AND NOT TO BE TIDIED AWAY. PowerFramework.Contracts declares zero
      project references by design, because the contracts project is the boundary definition and
      carries no behaviour of its own. The consequence is that the numeric agreement between the
      RetCode enum in Proto/common.v1.proto and the ported constants in
      PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel/RetCode.cs has no compile-time edge anywhere else in the tree.

      Kernel is referenced here so the fidelity tests can read their expectations FROM the Kernel
      constants rather than restating literals, which makes this project the single place where
      that otherwise review-time-only invariant is enforced mechanically. It is also why this
      project is sequenced behind Kernel.

      Scope is deliberately limited to these two: no service project and no other shared library
      is referenced, because a shared test project reaching into a service would invert the
      layering that keeps the published contracts the only cross-service coupling.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel\PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
